{
  "id": "6-11-sound-8-lab-a-settings-screen-that-remembers",
  "title": "6.11.8 Lab: A settings screen that remembers",
  "description": "A sound options screen: up and down step the level, M mutes, and both are saved with storeItem when they change and loaded with getItem when the game starts, so the game remembers them.",
  "type": "lesson",
  "estimateMins": 20,
  "category": "Unit 2: moSHion: Applied Game Development",
  "unit": "6.11 Sound and Music",
  "preview": "moshion",
  "week": 19,
  "slos": [
    "SLO-2",
    "SLO-3"
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "Change the level and the mute",
      "instructions": "The starter is your 6.11.7 game with a fifth screen, `'settings'`: O on the title opens it, and O there goes back. The orange basket stays at the bottom of every screen, this one too: a sprite is drawn on every frame until the sketch removes it, and this game keeps it. That is expected. It shows `level` (how loud everything is, 0 to 10) and whether the sound is on. Two variables hold them, `let level = 5;` and `let muted = false;`, and `applySound()` turns them into sound for everything at once with `masterVolume(muted ? 0 : level / 10);` (6.11.6). `soundChanged()` calls `applySound()` and plays the chime as a sample, so the player hears the new level.\n\nThis `level` is not the one from 6.11.4. There it was the tune's own volume, and M flipped `masterVolume()` between 0 and 1 itself. Here `level` is how loud everything is, and only `applySound()` sets `masterVolume`, from `level` and `muted`. So 6.11.4's M would undo itself here: `soundChanged()` runs `applySound()` on the same press and sets the volume straight back. Here M flips `muted`, and `applySound()` does the rest.\n\n`settingsScreen()` runs every frame on that screen and so far has only the O key. Above that line, add the controls: up raises `level` by 1 (only while it is below 10), down lowers it by 1 (only while it is above 0), and M flips `muted` with `muted = !muted;`. Each one then calls `soundChanged();`. Use `kb.presses`, so one press is one step (6.3.3). Run it, click the canvas, press O, and try them.",
      "hints": [
        "`if (kb.presses('up') && level < 10) { level += 1; soundChanged(); }`, then the same shape for down and for M.",
        "`!muted` is the opposite of `muted`: `true` becomes `false`, and `false` becomes `true`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "Save each change",
      "instructions": "Press Stop, then Run again: the level is back at 5 and the sound is on. A player expects a setting to stay. Save it once, when it changes (6.5.3). `soundChanged()` runs after every change, so at its end add:\n\n```js\nstoreItem('soundLevel', level);\nstoreItem('soundMuted', muted);\n```\n\n`storeItem` keeps a value under a name after the sketch stops. `'soundLevel'` and `'soundMuted'` are those names: any names work, as long as step 3 reads back the same ones. Nothing reads them back yet; that is step 3.",
      "hints": [
        "Both lines go inside `soundChanged()`, after `chime.play();`.",
        "Saving in `draw()` would write 60 times a second a value that changes only when a key is pressed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "Load them when the game starts",
      "instructions": "`setup()` calls `loadSettings()` before `applySound()`, and `loadSettings()` is empty. `getItem(name)` gives back exactly what was saved under that name, a number for `level` and `true` or `false` for `muted`, or `null` if nothing was ever saved (6.5.5, 6.5.6). So use what came back only when it is not `null`:\n\n```js\nconst savedLevel = getItem('soundLevel');\nif (savedLevel !== null) level = savedLevel;\n```\n\nand the same for `muted`. Why not `level = getItem('soundLevel') || 5;`, the way 6.5.6 did a first run? `||` picks the 5 whenever the left side counts as false, and a saved level of `0` counts as false just like `null`: a player who turned the sound all the way down would come back to 5. `!== null` keeps the 0. Run it, change the level, mute it, press Stop and Run: the options come back as you left them.",
      "hints": [
        "Two `getItem`s, each followed by an `if (... !== null)`.",
        "On the very first run both give back `null`, so `level` stays 5 and `muted` stays false."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "requirements": [
